// AAMC Question Bank Flashcards — CARS: original reasoning-skill questions (strategy items and original mini-passages)
// built from the question types in the AAMC CARS Question Pack, Vol. 1. No AAMC passages, questions or explanations are reproduced.
// Format: {s: tag, disc: cars, aamc: CMP | RWT | RBT, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, src}
const QB_AAMC_CARS = [
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Most histories credit the defeat of a 1960s urban freeway to one charismatic activist. Yet city records show that neighborhood associations had been petitioning, mapping and lobbying for a decade before she arrived. The freeway was stopped, I argue, because that long groundwork was already in place. Which statement best expresses the author\'s main point?',choices:['The freeway was stopped mainly by years of local organizing that preceded the famous activist','A single charismatic activist was chiefly responsible for stopping the freeway','Highway planners of the 1960s ignored the concerns of city residents','Neighborhood associations became more effective than activists in later decades'],ans:0,exp:'A main-point question asks for the author\'s **own conclusion**, which here is signaled by "I argue." The tempting distractor restates the "standard story" the author sets out to correct.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q1'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'On a CARS question asking what a word means "as it is used in the passage," the most reliable approach is to:',choices:['pick the most common dictionary definition of the word','substitute each choice into the sentence and use the contrast the passage sets up','choose the most technical or sophisticated meaning offered','select the meaning that best fits the passage\'s general topic'],ans:1,exp:'Meaning-in-context questions are answered by **substitution** and by the contrast the author draws (e.g., a term opposed to "understanding" means intuition). The everyday or literal meaning is the classic trap, because CARS words are often used in a specialized sense.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q2'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Admirers like to repeat that a baroque composer drafted all six of his concertos in a single week. It is true that he worked quickly. Yet the only source for the tale is a pupil\'s memoir written fifty years later, and the surviving manuscripts show corrections in three different inks. The author\'s attitude toward the "single week" story is best described as:',choices:['neutral, since the author reports the story without judging it','accepting, since the author grants that the composer worked quickly','skeptical, since the author finds the supporting evidence weak','hostile, since the author calls the composer\'s admirers dishonest'],ans:2,exp:'Attitude comes from the **qualifiers and counterevidence** the author marshals: a late secondhand source and multi-ink revisions both undercut the story. Conceding a minor point ("he worked quickly") does not make the author accepting.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q3'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A naturalist claims that a forest\'s recovery after a major fire was driven almost entirely by wind-blown seeds arriving from distant, unburned stands. Which of the following, if true, would NOT be consistent with this claim?',choices:['Seedlings first appeared along the windward edges of the burn','The species that recovered first all had light, winged seeds','Recovery was fastest in years with strong prevailing winds','Most new trees sprouted from roots that survived underground'],ans:3,exp:'For a NOT-consistent question, **test each choice against the claim** and find the one that contradicts it: trees regrowing from surviving roots means recovery did not depend on distant seeds. The other details all support wind dispersal, which is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q4'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A garden designer argues that the finest cottage gardens look as though no one planned them: plants self-seed where they please, paths wander, and nothing is clipped into shape. Which garden would the designer most likely admire?',choices:['A plot where poppies and foxgloves have seeded freely along an unedged path','A famous formal garden with perfectly symmetrical hedges and parterres','A carefully curated border in which each plant was chosen for its rare color','A minimalist courtyard of raked gravel arranged around three chosen stones'],ans:0,exp:'To apply an author\'s ideal to a new case, **identify the implied goal** (unplanned spontaneity) and pick the example that embodies it. The famous or curated gardens are traps because they reflect deliberate selection and control.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q5'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'When a CARS question asks which passage detail provides the strongest support for a claim, the best answer is usually the detail that:',choices:['uses the same key words as the claim, even if its meaning differs','most directly shows the trait the claim attributes to its subject','comes from a direct quotation of the person under discussion','appears near the end of the passage, where conclusions tend to sit'],ans:1,exp:'Strongest-support questions reward the fact that **most directly demonstrates** the claimed trait. A quotation can sound relevant yet actually imply the opposite, so check what it shows rather than whose voice it is in.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q6'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A chef famous for experimental dishes once said, "I am still learning to cook an egg." Throughout her career, the passage explains, she aimed not to reproduce classic recipes faithfully but to reveal what a single ingredient could become. In context, her remark most likely means that she:',choices:['had never mastered the basic techniques of classical cooking','planned to publish a revised cookbook devoted to eggs','saw even simple ingredients as holding undiscovered possibilities','felt that her skills were declining as she grew older'],ans:2,exp:'Interpret a quoted remark **through the author\'s characterization** of the speaker\'s aims (revealing what ingredients can become). Literal readings about lacking skill, future books or aging ignore that framing.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q7'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage defines an approach to architectural criticism as one that studies a building\'s spatial structure, and it explicitly sets aside the architect\'s biography and the public\'s reactions. Asked what this approach focuses on, a test-taker should choose:',choices:['the architect\'s life and the circumstances behind each design','the way occupants and the public respond to each building','a balance of biography, public response and spatial form','the organization of space within the buildings themselves'],ans:3,exp:'Find the **author\'s own definition** of the approach\'s object. Concerns the author explicitly sets aside (biography, audience response) are traps, even though they are mentioned prominently.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q8'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Some hospitals label any bedside conversation about a patient\'s values an "ethics consult." But a conversation that merely records preferences is not ethics consultation; consultation requires weighing competing obligations and recommending a course of action. This argument assumes that:',choices:['some kinds of bedside discussion are more valuable for ethical decisions than others','recording patient preferences is nothing more than useless paperwork','hospitals should stop asking patients about their values entirely','only trained philosophers are able to weigh competing obligations'],ans:0,exp:'Excluding some activities from a valued category **assumes a ranking**: some discussions contribute more to ethical decision-making than others. The "nothing more than useless" choice overstates the author, who never dismisses recording preferences.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q9'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'On a CARS question asking which of the author\'s claims receives the MOST support in the passage, the best answer is usually the claim that:',choices:['is most obviously true, even if the author simply concedes it','the author backs with reasons, examples or extended argument','appears in the opening sentence of the passage','the author attributes to respected outside experts'],ans:1,exp:'"Most support" means **most argued for**, so look for reasons and examples behind a claim. Truisms the author merely grants can be true in the passage yet receive no support at all.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q10'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'Some food critics judge a restaurant by interviewing the chef about her inspirations. I reject this method: it tells us how the menu came to be, not what actually arrives on the plate. The author rejects the interview method because it:',choices:['cannot provide any information of value to diners at all','relies on chefs who usually misrepresent their intentions','describes how the food was created rather than the food itself','takes more time than tasting the dishes would require'],ans:2,exp:'Use the **author\'s stated reason**: the method addresses the process of creation, not the product. The extreme version ("cannot provide any information") goes beyond what the author claims.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q11'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A historian of photography argues that a photograph should be read as an image, not as a message from its maker: what the frame shows matters more than what the person behind the camera meant to say. Which statement would the author most likely endorse?',choices:['A photo is best understood by learning the photographer\'s intentions','Letters by photographers are the key to interpreting their prints','A photo fails if viewers miss the message its maker intended','A photo may hold meanings its maker never consciously put there'],ans:3,exp:'Match the new statement to the **author\'s principle**: meaning lives in the image, not in the maker\'s intended message. The other choices all center intention, the view the author opposes.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q12'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A school of music analysis holds that a symphony should be judged only by its internal architecture: how themes are introduced, developed and resolved. To illustrate, its founder imagines an "ideal listener" who hears every motif and forgets nothing. Analysis, on this view, asks whether a work\'s structure holds together. This kind of analysis would most clearly have the capacity to distinguish between:',choices:['coherent and incoherent ways of developing a theme','one listener\'s hearing of a symphony and another\'s','symphonies by famous and by obscure composers','performances given in large halls and in small ones'],ans:0,exp:'When an approach is **defined by its object** (structure), its evaluative power applies to that object, so it can judge sound versus unsound thematic development. The ideal listener is an illustrative device, not the method\'s purpose, so comparing listeners\' hearings is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q13'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'When a CARS question asks which finding would most seriously CHALLENGE a theory, the best answer usually:',choices:['restates a limitation the theory\'s defenders already concede','contradicts one of the core principles the theory depends on','concerns a minor example the author mentions in passing','shows that people disagree about how to apply the theory'],ans:1,exp:'A serious challenge **contradicts a core principle**. A fact the theory already concedes (e.g., that readers vary) cannot challenge it, because the theory was built to accommodate it.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q14'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Many economists treat household cooking as unpaid labor best measured in hours and dollars. That view has its uses, but it misses what cooking produces: shared routines, inherited knowledge and a sense of belonging. Domestic cooking, I argue, should be understood as a cultural practice, not merely as work. Which statement best expresses the main idea?',choices:['Household cooking is best measured as unpaid labor in hours and dollars','Economists have ignored household cooking altogether in their research','Domestic cooking is a cultural practice whose value exceeds its labor','Inherited recipes are the most valuable thing a family can pass on'],ans:2,exp:'The main idea is the **thesis the passage builds to**, here flagged by "I argue." The labor view is introduced only to be qualified, so choosing it picks a position the author dismisses.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q15'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'On a Roman-numeral CARS question that asks which statements are true "according to the passage," the most reliable method is to:',choices:['keep any statement that sounds like something the author would believe','accept the statements that best fit the passage\'s general topic','pick the answer choice that includes the greatest number of numerals','check each statement separately against what the text actually says'],ans:3,exp:'"According to the passage" demands **textual confirmation for each statement** on its own. Plausible-sounding statements the passage never makes are the main trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q16'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A dance scholar argues that criticism has gone astray by borrowing methods from the sciences: counting steps per minute, tracing choreography to its economic setting, or charting how styles evolved. Real insight, she says, comes from attending closely to the dance itself. Which project would she most likely approve?',choices:['A close study of how one ballet\'s movements build and release tension','A statistical tally of how many leaps appear in each modern ballet','An analysis of how ticket prices shaped the subjects of ballets','A family tree tracing which styles descended from earlier ones'],ans:0,exp:'Apply the author\'s view by **eliminating the approaches she names as failed borrowings** (counting, economic context, evolutionary tracing). Only the close study of a single work fits her method.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q17'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A passage claims that a novelist "bends time" but never explains what the phrase means. Which addition would most effectively clarify the claim?',choices:['Defining the phrase with an unusual new term the author coins','A specific scene in which the novelist jumps ahead and then back','A new assertion that the novelist was the first to bend time','A remark that many readers find the novelist hard to follow'],ans:1,exp:'A vague claim is clarified best by a **concrete example** from a specific work. Coining another term or adding a fresh unsupported assertion leaves the original claim just as unclear.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q18'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Why does folk knowledge of medicinal herbs matter? Not, the author insists, because it is a crude early version of pharmacology waiting to be confirmed in the lab. Folk herbalism matters because it is its own kind of knowing, rooted in seasons, places and relationships, that laboratory science neither captures nor replaces. According to the author, folk herbal knowledge is important chiefly because it:',choices:['often anticipates findings that pharmacologists later confirm','is valuable and well worth preserving for future generations','offers a distinct form of knowledge that science does not supply','connects communities closely to the land on which they live'],ans:2,exp:'Prefer the answer that captures the **author\'s specific emphasis**: a distinct kind of knowledge, separate from science. "Valuable and worth preserving" is a broad paraphrase the author would not deny but does not make the point, and "anticipates findings" is the view the author rejects.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q19'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A CARS question says a new finding is "consistent with" one claim in the passage and is "evidence for" another. What distinguishes these two relationships?',choices:['They mean the same thing, so either label can describe any finding','Consistency requires proof, while evidence only requires no conflict','A finding is evidence for a claim only if the author cites it directly','Consistency means no conflict, while evidence actively raises support'],ans:3,exp:'A finding is **consistent with** a claim if it does not contradict it, but it is **evidence for** a claim only if it makes the claim more likely. Treating the two as interchangeable is the trap on Roman-numeral "relationship" questions.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q20'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author argues that public parks should not be justified as health clinics or economic engines. Parks have their own purposes, such as play, rest and chance encounters, and should be judged by how well they serve those ends, not by how well they substitute for other services. Elsewhere she distinguishes parks designed as artworks from parks designed for use. A landscape architect writes, "A park is not a cheaper gym." This remark most closely parallels which assertion?',choices:['Parks have their own aims and should not be judged as substitutes for other services','Some parks are designed as works of art while others are designed for daily use','Parks improve the physical health of the people who live near them','Parks contribute to the economic vitality of the districts around them'],ans:0,exp:'Match an outside quotation by the **function of its claim**: "not a cheaper gym" denies that a park is a substitute for something else, which parallels the distinct-purpose assertion. The art-versus-use distinction shares surface vocabulary about park design but makes a different point.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q21'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'At a music festival, sponsors pay least to have a logo on a banner, more to have the announcer mention the brand by name, and most to have performers actually use the product on stage. Organizers say the price of each option tracks how persuasive it is. By this reasoning, which placement would be most effective?',choices:['A giant illuminated logo spanning the back of the main stage','A headliner drinking the sponsor\'s soda between songs on stage','An announcer thanking the sponsor by name before each set','A banner with the sponsor\'s logo hung above the festival gate'],ans:1,exp:'If **price signals effectiveness**, the top-priced tier (a performer using the product) ranks highest. A giant logo is visually prominent but is still simple visual exposure, the cheapest tier.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q22'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A passage reports survey results on a controversial marketing practice, calls the critics\' objections "groundless," and spends its final paragraph rebutting calls for regulation. The author\'s attitude toward the practice is best described as:',choices:['neutral, because the author mainly reports research findings','skeptical, because the author acknowledges the critics\' views','favorable, because the author defends it with loaded wording','hostile, because the author discusses calls for regulation'],ans:2,exp:'**Loaded wording** ("groundless") and a rebuttal structure reveal a favorable stance. Reporting research does not make a passage neutral when the whole piece defends one side.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q23'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A study of museum audio guides found that visitors who knew a guide was sponsored by an art dealer trusted its commentary less and resisted its suggestions. Among teenagers the effect was weaker, since many simply enjoyed the guide\'s music. Overall, the author concludes, awareness of sponsorship makes visitors wary. Which statement best expresses the author\'s claim?',choices:['Teenagers enjoy audio guides more than other visitors do','Sponsorship has no effect on how visitors respond to guides','Audio guides should play music to hold teenagers\' attention','Knowing a guide is sponsored makes visitors more resistant to it'],ans:3,exp:'Distinguish the **general claim** from a subgroup finding: the conclusion concerns visitors overall. The teenager detail is true of only one group, so stating it as the author\'s claim is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q24'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A study concludes that a diet app changes what people eat, basing this conclusion solely on interviews in which users describe their meals. The most serious weakness of the study is most likely that:',choices:['it relies on what users say they eat rather than on measured intake','the app may have been designed by a company with poor reviews','some users might have deleted the app after the interviews ended','interviews are usually more expensive to run than online surveys'],ans:0,exp:'A conclusion about behavior drawn only from **self-reports** is weak because what people say may differ from what they do. The other flaws are speculative, with no support in the description.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q25'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Researchers found that children respond to brands they already recognize with feelings of comfort and trust, and that this comfort drives their requests to parents. A popular cartoon then showed its hero eating cereal from a brand-new company, and sales of that cereal did not rise. Which explanation best fits the researchers\' findings?',choices:['Most children watching already owned a box of that cereal','The unfamiliar brand could not trigger the comfort that drives requests','Children did not notice the cereal box during the cartoon','Parents refused to buy any cereal advertised in cartoons'],ans:1,exp:'Explain the new outcome **using the stated mechanism**: comfort comes from familiarity, and a new brand lacks it. Explanations about ownership, attention or parents are not grounded in the findings.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q26'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A report praises a pesticide\'s safety record, describes opponents\' worries as overblown, and warns that new regulations would hurt farmers. Based on its framing alone, the report was most likely sponsored by:',choices:['an independent university team with no stake in the result','a government agency proposing the new regulations','a trade association representing pesticide manufacturers','an environmental group campaigning against the pesticide'],ans:2,exp:'Infer the source from its **framing and interests**: pro-industry claims plus opposition to regulation point to an industry group. Neutral scientists or regulators would frame the issue very differently.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q27'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Defenders of background music in stores argue that shoppers are not manipulated by it: everyone knows stores play music to set a mood, so no one is deceived. Which finding would most weaken the defenders\' argument?',choices:['Sales of all products rise slightly during the holiday season','Shoppers say they prefer stores that play no music at all','Music has no measurable effect on sales whether noticed or not','Music boosts sales, but the boost vanishes when shoppers are told its aim'],ans:3,exp:'If **awareness cancels the effect**, then the effect works only on shoppers who do not grasp it, which undercuts "no one is deceived." A finding of no effect at all would actually support the defenders.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q28'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A historian argues that early jazz records sold mainly to audiences seeking homegrown musicians, citing city shop records showing that 70 percent of records sold featured local bands. Suppose it were also shown that recording out-of-town bands was cheaper and more profitable for labels. Would this further the historian\'s argument?',choices:['Yes, because local bands dominated sales despite being the costlier option','No, because it concerns labels\' costs rather than audience demand','No, because it suggests labels preferred to promote out-of-town bands','Yes, because it shows labels cared mostly about short-term profits'],ans:0,exp:'Trace the **implication**: if labels had an incentive to push out-of-town bands yet local bands still dominated sales, demand for local music must have been even stronger. The "No" answers miss that the cost fact sharpens the evidence.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q29'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'On a CARS question asking which of the author\'s assertions is NOT supported by evidence in the passage, the best answer is usually a claim that:',choices:['the author backs with counts, figures or documented sources','the author simply states, often signaled by words like "evidently"','contradicts a view the author attributes to other scholars','the author repeats several times across the passage'],ans:1,exp:'**Separate data-backed claims from bare assertions**: words like "evidently" or "clearly" often introduce claims with no evidence behind them. Claims backed by counts are supported, even if they are not the main point.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q30'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'Critics often blame one city\'s planners for its sprawl. But sprawl was not unique to that city. Across the continent, cities of every size spread outward after the war as cars became affordable. The final sentence functions primarily to:',choices:['introduce a new argument the author develops later on','concede that critics of the planners were partly correct','support the claim that sprawl was not unique to that city','explain why that city\'s planners chose to favor car travel'],ans:2,exp:'For a function question, ask **what the sentence does for its neighbor**: it supplies evidence for the immediately preceding claim. Nothing is developed later, so "introduces a new argument" is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q31'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A question asks which part of a passage an outside theory is "logically consistent with." The best strategy is to match the part of the passage that:',choices:['discusses the same topic or time period as the outside theory','uses the most similar vocabulary to the outside theory','the author most strongly endorses, whatever its reasoning','shares the same cause-and-effect logic as the outside theory'],ans:3,exp:'Logical consistency is about **shared reasoning structure**, such as "isolation produces a particular kind of art." Matching on surface topic or vocabulary is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q32'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Drawing on ticket sales and newspaper reviews, the author shows that audiences in the 1920s eagerly attended homegrown plays and that critics praised such plays generously. Which item would one LEAST expect to find from that period?',choices:['A news editorial lamenting public indifference to homegrown plays','A letter complaining that critics praise local plays too freely','A theater ledger showing sold-out runs of a local drama','A playwright\'s diary noting packed houses on opening night'],ans:0,exp:'The "least expected" item is the one that **contradicts the author\'s findings**, here a complaint of public indifference. A complaint about overly generous reviews actually fits a warm reception.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q33'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'An author quotes contemporary complaints that audiences of the era were "far too easily pleased" and uses them to show that new works were warmly received. The author treats the complainers\' views as:',choices:['misguided opinions the author sets out to refute','credible testimony that supports the author\'s claim','biased remarks that must be excluded from the evidence','the main thesis that the passage was written to defend'],ans:1,exp:'Ask **how the author uses the cited views**: the complaints serve as evidence of a warm reception. Reading them as dismissed overlooks that the author relies on them.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q34'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'An author concludes that a 19th-century gardening magazine accurately tracked which plants were popular and that its readers preferred native plants to exotic imports. Which data would help evaluate these conclusions? I. Nursery sales records showing which plants actually sold. II. Surveys of readers\' plant preferences from the period. III. The average length of the magazine\'s articles.',choices:['I only','III only','I and II only','I, II and III'],ans:2,exp:'Useful data must **bear on a claim the author actually makes**: sales records test the magazine\'s accuracy and surveys test reader preference. Article length is a detail the author never argues about.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q35'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'When a CARS question asks for a passage\'s central thesis, the best answer is usually:',choices:['the most vivid example the author uses to make a point','a narrower claim offered in support of a larger point','the view of the scholar the author quotes at greatest length','the broad claim that every example and sub-point serves'],ans:3,exp:'The central thesis is the **claim everything else serves**. Supporting details and narrower sub-claims are true parts of the passage but not its thesis.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q36'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A teacher argues that classroom routines, such as greeting each student by name and waiting for one another to finish speaking, shape behavior better than punishments do. Routines work, she says, because they honor each person\'s dignity, while punishments work only through fear. Which reason for using routines would she most strongly endorse?',choices:['They show respect for each student as a person of worth','They require less effort from the teacher than discipline','They let the teacher avoid the risk of angry parents','They make the school day run on a predictable schedule'],ans:0,exp:'Tie the endorsed reason to the **passage\'s central contrast** (dignity versus fear). Lower effort, avoided risk or predictability may be side benefits, but they are not the value she defends.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q37'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'The author writes that a simple handshake has an almost alchemical power: it turns two strangers into parties bound by an agreement. The author most likely uses the word "alchemical" to:',choices:['claim that handshakes once played a role in medieval chemistry','highlight the hidden power that ordinary gestures can carry','suggest that agreements are illusions that soon dissolve','imply that handshakes have supernatural origins in ritual'],ans:1,exp:'Context shows the word is **figurative**, stressing the transforming power of an everyday convention. Literal or supernatural readings ignore that the author is describing an ordinary gesture.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q38'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A linguist defines a performative utterance as one in which saying the words is itself doing the act, rather than describing an action or prompting someone else to act. Which of the following is a performative utterance?',choices:['"Could you close the window before you leave?"','"Please remember to sign the form by Friday."','"I hereby resign from my post on the committee."','"Will the meeting start at noon tomorrow?"'],ans:2,exp:'Apply the definition strictly: saying "I hereby resign" **is** the resignation. Requests and questions only prompt another person to act, which the definition excludes.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q39'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage on a secular topic calls a practice "sacred" after several paragraphs linking it to reverence and mutual respect. Asked why the author uses that word, the best answer:',choices:['cites a divine law that the practice was believed to fulfill','points to holy persons who first performed the practice','notes that the practice began within a religious order','follows the author\'s own link between the practice and respect'],ans:3,exp:'Follow the **author\'s stated linkage**: the practice is "sacred" because it embodies reverence and respect. Literal religious explanations import meanings the passage never supplies.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q40'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A legal scholar notes that when a judge says, "I sentence you to five years," the words do not report a sentence; they impose it. This example most clearly implies that:',choices:['saying something and doing something can be the same act','judges often conceal their true reasoning behind formulas','legal language is necessary for any action to take effect','spoken sentences carry less weight than written ones'],ans:0,exp:'The example shows that **speech can itself be action**. Claims about concealment or necessity go beyond what the single example implies.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q41'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Two travelers described a young republic. The first praised its citizens\' commercial energy and predicted that it alone would sustain self-government. The second admired that energy too but warned that self-interest could not sustain free institutions without civic habits. A study finds that towns with booming trade but no civic associations saw their local governments collapse. This finding supports:',choices:['the first traveler, since the towns showed commercial energy','the second traveler, since self-interest alone did not suffice','both travelers equally, since both admired commerce','neither traveler, since neither discussed local government'],ans:1,exp:'Ask **which view the example confirms**: trade without civic habits failed, exactly as the second traveler warned. The towns\' commercial energy is the trap, since it is the very factor the first traveler wrongly thought sufficient.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q42'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage compares two observers and explains that one was more accurate because his views were shaped by direct experience and long-held convictions. Asked why his account was more accurate, the best answer:',choices:['names a trait the author attributes to the less accurate observer','offers a plausible reason the reader could infer from history','restates the author\'s explanation rooted in experience and conviction','notes which observer the passage discusses at greater length'],ans:2,exp:'Use the **author\'s stated reason** rather than an outside inference. Traits the author assigns to the other observer are a common trap on comparison questions.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q43'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'An anthropologist uses the phrase "temperament of a village" to mean the moral dispositions its people carry in their hearts, as distinct from its wealth, its formal doctrines or its trade. Which of the following best exemplifies a village\'s temperament in this sense?',choices:['The village\'s ranking as the richest in the valley','A written philosophy taught in the village school','The villagers\' eagerness to open new trade routes','The villagers\' deep habit of caring for the sick'],ans:3,exp:'Apply the **defined term** exactly: a moral disposition of the heart. Wealth, doctrine and trade are discussed under different headings, so they do not qualify even though they describe the village.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q44'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'On a Roman-numeral question asking which factors the passage says challenged a belief, a statement should be kept only if:',choices:['the passage explicitly names it as challenging that belief','it is historically plausible that it challenged the belief','the passage mentions it anywhere, even as supporting the belief','it would weaken the belief if a reader thought it through'],ans:0,exp:'Keep only what the passage **explicitly ties to the challenge**. A factor the passage mentions as confirming the belief is the trap, because mere mention is not the same role.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q45'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'One economist saw automation as pure promise: higher output and shorter workweeks. Another agreed that output would rise but warned that displaced workers might lose both income and status. Compared with the first, the second economist\'s outlook was:',choices:['equally optimistic, since both expected output to rise','more pessimistic, since she foresaw troubling side effects','extremely pessimistic, since she rejected automation entirely','more optimistic, since she considered workers\' welfare'],ans:1,exp:'Compare outlooks **relatively**: seeing troubling implications makes the second view more pessimistic than one that sees only promise. The extreme choice fails because she never rejects automation.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q46'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage reports that a city\'s bus ridership fell 15 percent after fares doubled and that some former riders switched to bicycles. Which conclusions are justified by the passage? I. Ridership declined after the fare increase. II. Some riders turned to bicycles. III. The bus system faces a swift collapse.',choices:['I only','III only','I and II only','I, II and III'],ans:2,exp:'Keep statements **directly supported** by the passage and reject exaggerated predictions. A 15 percent drop says nothing about a swift collapse.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q47'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A passage about job training repeatedly asks how one country can keep pace with its rivals abroad, while listing workers, firms and schools as contributing factors. Whose competitiveness does the author mainly worry about?',choices:['that of individual workers seeking higher pay','that of firms trying to hold down training costs','that of schools competing for public funding','that of the nation as a whole in the world economy'],ans:3,exp:'Identify the **passage\'s frame**, which is national competitiveness. Workers, firms and schools are factors the author lists within that frame, not the group whose standing is at stake.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q48'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A report states that farmers who adopt drip irrigation earn more than farmers who do not. Which finding would most clearly contradict this claim?',choices:['Farmers who adopted drip irrigation earned less than non-adopters','Drip irrigation is more common in dry regions than in wet ones','Some farmers adopted drip irrigation only after earning more','Drip systems require more maintenance than flood irrigation'],ans:0,exp:'The **clearest contradiction** is the direct opposite of the reported effect. Reverse causation or maintenance costs are tangential, since they do not deny that adopters earn more.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q49'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'When a CARS question asks what the author advocates, the strongest clue is usually:',choices:['a trend the author describes in neutral, factual terms','a recommendation the author repeats, often with "should"','the position of the expert the author quotes first','the topic that takes up the most space in the passage'],ans:1,exp:'Advocacy shows up in **prescriptive language** such as "should" or "must," especially when repeated. Things the author merely describes are not recommendations.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q50'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A sociologist sorts careers into three tracks: a high track of broad skills that open many doors, a low track with no training at all, and a middle track in which workers receive some training but stay stuck below the top. Which worker fits the middle track?',choices:['A nurse whose broad clinical training lets her move into many fields','A day laborer hired each morning with no training of any kind','A machine operator trained only on one plant\'s outdated equipment','An engineer who rises to lead a firm after years of varied work'],ans:2,exp:'Apply the **coined category** precisely: some training that does not transfer upward. The other examples fit the top or bottom tiers, which is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q51'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A bakery owner believes quality rises when every worker, from the mixer to the cashier, shows expertise and takes responsibility for the final loaf. Which practice would best promote this outcome?',choices:['Installing cameras so managers can monitor each workstation','Sharing a portion of yearly profits with all employees','Giving a bonus to the worker with the fastest daily output','Having workers at each station share quality tips with one another'],ans:3,exp:'Match the **stated mechanism**: expertise and shared responsibility at every stage. Monitoring or profit-sharing may motivate workers, but in other ways than the owner describes.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q52'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A hospital administrator argues that hospitals thrive by investing heavily in training their nurses and by building long-term relationships with them, rather than by hiring cheaply and replacing staff often. Her hospital now faces a problem: rival hospitals lure away newly trained nurses with higher pay. Which response best fits her strategy?',choices:['Keep training nurses but require a multiyear service pledge or repayment','Cut spending on supplies so salaries can match the rivals\' offers','Stop training and instead hire experienced nurses from rival hospitals','Shorten training so that less money is lost when nurses leave early'],ans:0,exp:'A solution "in line with" a strategy must **preserve its defining features**: continued investment plus a long-term commitment. Cutting costs elsewhere to match wages is not part of her model, and the other options abandon investment altogether.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q53'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A passage claims (1) that firms should invest more in worker training now and (2) that training by itself does not create good jobs. Which conclusion follows from the two claims taken together?',choices:['Firms should stop investing in training until good jobs exist','Some well-trained workers may still fail to benefit financially','Training is the single most important source of good jobs','Workers should pay for their own training to protect firms'],ans:1,exp:'**Combine the claims**: if training is urged but cannot guarantee good jobs, some trained workers may not gain. The other choices follow from only one claim, or contradict one.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q54'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Habit theory, the author says, is valuable because it explains why people handle familiar tasks smoothly: past repetitions shape how new instances are carried out. The author adds that the theory cannot tell us whether any given habit is a good one. According to the author, the theory matters mainly because it:',choices:['can determine whether a particular habit is beneficial','proves that every habit is formed through repetition','explains how past experience shapes familiar tasks','lets researchers predict which habits will be broken'],ans:2,exp:'The author values the theory for its **explanatory role**. Judging whether a habit is good is exactly what the passage says the theory cannot do, which makes that choice the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q55'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Emergency departments use standard triage protocols so that staff need not reason from scratch for every patient with chest pain. This practice assumes that:',choices:['staff will recall every past chest-pain case exactly','each protocol can be applied to only one patient','chest pain always has the same underlying cause','prior knowledge helps staff handle new similar cases'],ans:3,exp:'A reusable script **assumes prior knowledge transfers** to new instances of a familiar situation. Extreme claims about exact recall or single use contradict the purpose of a protocol.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q56'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A theory holds that we understand stories by fitting them to familiar templates, and that recall errors in which the template fills in gaps are a normal by-product of this process. Readers of a story about a restaurant meal later "remember" a character paying the bill, though the story never mentioned it. This observation best supports which claim?',choices:['Template-driven recall errors are a normal part of comprehension','Readers who misremember details have weak reading comprehension','Stories about restaurants are harder to remember than others','People rarely rely on templates when reading unfamiliar stories'],ans:0,exp:'The invented detail is exactly what the **theory predicts**: the restaurant template fills the gap. The other claims are never made by the theory, and one contradicts it.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q57'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'When a CARS question asks for the most reasonable conclusion from a single example in the passage, the best answer usually:',choices:['adds specific motives, such as deliberate intent, to the example','generalizes only as far as the example itself can support','extends the example to every similar case in all contexts','restates a claim the author makes elsewhere on another topic'],ans:1,exp:'A reasonable conclusion **goes no further than the example**. Adding unsupported specifics like deliberate intent, or universal generalizations, is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q58'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A psychologist defines "assimilation" as the moment a child judges a new object to fit an existing category, as when a toddler calls a zebra a horse. Which procedure would best test whether assimilation has occurred?',choices:['Seeing whether the child invents a new category for the object','Measuring how long the child looks at the new object','Asking whether the child places the object in a known category','Recording whether the child can name the object\'s parts'],ans:2,exp:'To **operationalize** a term, test exactly what the definition specifies: fit to an existing category. Creating a new category contradicts the definition.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q59'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A theory holds that people understand what they read by activating background knowledge about the topic. A teacher who accepts this theory would most likely:',choices:['drill students on phonics before any reading assignment','time students to improve their reading speed each week','assign longer texts to build students\' reading stamina','build students\' knowledge of a topic before they read about it'],ans:3,exp:'Apply the theory\'s **mechanism** (activated background knowledge) to practice. Phonics, speed and stamina are real skills but are unrelated to that mechanism.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q60'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A CARS question asks what the author asserts about BOTH of two groups. The best approach is to:',choices:['keep only a claim the passage applies to each group','pick a claim the passage makes about either group','choose the claim the author stresses most strongly','select the claim that best distinguishes the groups'],ans:0,exp:'"Both" requires a statement **true of each group**. A claim true of only one group is the standard trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q61'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A historian argues that a medieval town declined because its river silted up and cut off trade, citing port records that show ship traffic collapsing. In passing, she guesses that the town\'s mayor at the time was a wool merchant. If it were found that the mayor was in fact a baker, how would this affect her argument?',choices:['It would seriously weaken it, since it undermines her sources','It would leave it intact, since it touches only an aside','It would strengthen it, since bakers relied on river trade','It would refute it, since it shows her research was careless'],ans:1,exp:'Ask whether the discovery **touches the argument\'s support**. The mayor\'s trade was an incidental guess, so the port-record argument stands; overrating a side remark is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q62'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'When a CARS question asks which discovery would most WEAKEN an author\'s argument, the best answer typically:',choices:['concerns an interesting side issue the author raises briefly','adds a new detail the author did not have room to mention','contradicts a core premise on which the conclusion rests','shows that other scholars dislike the author\'s conclusion'],ans:2,exp:'The strongest weakener **attacks a core premise**. Discoveries about side issues may be interesting but leave the main argument standing.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q63'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A critic shows that a later group of architects, claiming to follow a modernist founder, built ornate facades that his writings expressly condemned. If the critic is right, which statement must be true?',choices:['All later followers of a founder distort the founder\'s ideas','Modernist architecture has been abandoned by most architects','Ornate facades are always inconsistent with modern design','Some work done in a founder\'s name can contradict his ideas'],ans:3,exp:'A "must be true" answer is the **minimal claim** the passage proves: one case establishes "some." Sweeping words like "all" and "always" go far beyond a single example.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q64'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A passage notes that a poet wrote in the 1600s and that a school of critics arose in the 1900s that praised and imitated him. Which influence is impossible, given the passage?',choices:['The 1900s critics shaped the poet\'s choice of themes','The poet shaped the style of the 1900s critics','Earlier poets shaped the poet\'s style and themes','The poet shaped writers of the 1700s and 1800s'],ans:0,exp:'Use the **timeline**: a later group cannot influence an earlier writer. The other influences are merely unproven, not impossible.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q65'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'An objector says, "Patients should defer to doctors, because otherwise patients could make any choice at all." The author replies, "Doctors could also make any choice at all." The reply is relevant because it:',choices:['shows that patients reliably decide better than their doctors','exposes the objection\'s hidden premise that doctors choose better','proves that no one should make medical decisions at all','changes the subject to the training that doctors receive'],ans:1,exp:'A rebuttal is relevant if it **targets an unstated assumption**: the objection works only if doctors decide better. Dismissing the reply as off-topic misses that hidden premise.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q66'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author argues that a person can never truly hand over moral responsibility by promising to obey a leader, because the person always remains free to break the promise. If some people were literally unable to break such promises, this would:',choices:['support the claim, since the promise would always be kept','have no bearing, since the claim concerns leaders only','challenge the claim, since its key premise would fail','support the claim, since it shows promises are binding'],ans:2,exp:'Find the **premise the claim rests on** (freedom to break the promise) and see that the hypothetical removes it. Reading the case as support confuses keeping a promise with retaining responsibility.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q67'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'An essayist holds that no institution can take over a person\'s duty to judge right and wrong. Which belief best fits this view?',choices:['Laws should be obeyed because obedience preserves social order','Experts should decide moral questions because they know more','Tradition should guide conduct because it has stood the test of time','Each person retains final authority over his or her own conscience'],ans:3,exp:'The theme places **moral legitimacy in the individual**. Views that make order, expertise or tradition the reason to obey hand judgment to an outside authority.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q68'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'An argument runs: "Someone must decide; officials are no more reliable than I am; so I may as well decide myself." Which hypothetical most directly challenges a step of this argument?',choices:['Officials are shown to be far more reliable than individuals','Individuals are shown to enjoy making their own decisions','Officials are shown to earn more than most individuals','Individuals are shown to decide faster than officials'],ans:0,exp:'Target the **specific step**: the conclusion depends on officials being no more reliable. Facts about enjoyment, pay or speed leave that premise untouched.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q69'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A philosopher argues that people should follow their own judgment rather than law as such. She notes that driving on the right is still wise, not because the law says so, but because everyone else does it. The fact that most people obey traffic laws would:',choices:['weaken her thesis, since people obey the law as such','neither support nor weaken it, since she already explains such cases','strengthen her thesis, since it shows people use their judgment','weaken her thesis, since it shows laws are truly necessary'],ans:1,exp:'If the author has **already accounted for** a type of case, it is not new evidence either way. Treating an addressed example as fresh support or refutation is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q70'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'An author says that the experiences of two groups "differ in important ways yet overlap in others." Which assumption underlies this view?',choices:['The two experiences are entirely different from each other','The two experiences always coincide in every respect','The two experiences share at least some similarities','The two experiences cannot be compared in any way'],ans:2,exp:'"Overlap" signals **some similarity**, a moderate claim. Absolutes such as "entirely different" or "always coincide" contradict the author\'s balanced wording.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q71'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Some critics claim that frontier diaries share a distinctive literary style. I find little evidence of that. What unites these diaries is their writers\' effort to build a life within a landscape that seemed indifferent to them. Which characteristic does the author attribute to the diaries?',choices:['A shared and distinctive literary style','A tendency to celebrate the frontier\'s beauty','A focus on the politics of frontier settlement','A struggle to make a home in an uncaring land'],ans:3,exp:'Take the **author\'s own defining description**, not a view attributed to others. The distinctive-style claim belongs to critics the author rejects.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q72'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'When a CARS question asks what the author asserts about a particular writer, the best answer is usually:',choices:['the explicit claim the author makes about that writer','a bolder claim that would follow if the author\'s praise is true','a claim the author makes about writers in general','an inference about the writer\'s influence on later writers'],ans:0,exp:'"Asserts" means **stated outright**. Overextensions, such as saying a writer created a new form when the author only praised one innovation, are the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q73'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'The author says that in one composer\'s opera a storm is an archetypal symbol of fate, while in another composer\'s opera it is merely a device to strand the lovers. She adds that only a composer raised by the sea could have scored the storm as the first did. Which does the author assert? I. The storm is an archetypal symbol for the first composer. II. The storm is a plot device for the second composer. III. Any composer could have scored the storm as the first did.',choices:['I only','I and II only','II and III only','I, II and III'],ans:1,exp:'Check each statement against the text: I and II are **stated**, while III is **contradicted** by "only a composer raised by the sea." Keeping the contradicted statement is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q74'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage quotes a writer who preferred the novel to the epic because "the novel is still young and pliable." Asked why the writer preferred the novel, the best answer:',choices:['says that the epic was the newer and more flexible form','draws on a quotation from a different writer in the passage','says the novel, as a newer form, could still be reshaped','offers a reason that the reader finds personally persuasive'],ans:2,exp:'Use the **quoted reason** itself: the novel\'s youth made it flexible. Reversing which genre was newer, or borrowing another writer\'s quote, are the classic traps.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q75'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Gardening clubs, the author argues, are not mere hobbies. They carry deep civic weight, organizing neighbors much as town meetings once did. Along the way she notes their roots in Victorian horticulture and their role in wartime food drives. What is the passage\'s primary message?',choices:['Gardening clubs grew out of Victorian horticultural societies','Gardening clubs played a part in wartime food drives','Gardening clubs have entirely replaced town meetings','Gardening clubs hold real civic importance for communities'],ans:3,exp:'The primary message is the **author\'s strongest stated point**. History and wartime roles are subtopics, and "entirely replaced" overstates the comparison.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q76'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Writing about why people climb mountains, the author coins the word "summitward" to describe an urge felt by mountaineers, casual hikers and armchair travelers alike. The author most likely coins this word in order to:',choices:['name an urge broadly enough to include all kinds of people','reveal that she is herself an accomplished mountaineer','predict that climbing will soon become far more popular','criticize armchair travelers for never leaving home'],ans:0,exp:'An unusual coined term often serves to be **inclusive and generic**, covering people regardless of experience. Reading it as personal testimony or prediction adds claims the author never makes.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q77'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'An author writes that dramatic contests once held "their classical position" as festivals honoring the gods and the city. The phrase most clearly reveals that the author:',choices:['feels nostalgic grief over the decline of ancient festivals','understands the civic and religious role drama once served','disapproves of mixing theater with religion and politics','believes modern theater is less exciting than ancient drama'],ans:1,exp:'Ask **what the phrase shows**: knowledge of the historical function the contests served. Emotional attitudes like grief or disapproval are not revealed by the wording.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q78'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author argues that concert rituals, such as standing ovations, encores and shared silence, move all attendees, whether devoted fans or not, to a sense of communion. Which finding would most weaken this claim?',choices:['Most attendees prefer encores to standing ovations','Performers feel the communion more than audiences do','Only longtime devotees of the genre report feeling communion','Concert attendance has risen steadily over the past decade'],ans:2,exp:'The claim explicitly includes casual attendees, so a finding that **only devotees** feel communion contradicts it. Preferences or performer-versus-audience intensity do not touch the claim.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q79'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'A writer on chess says that "for a serious player, resigning a game is a small death." This statement most likely emphasizes:',choices:['that serious players face real physical danger at the board','that resigning a game is morally wrong for serious players','that chess players should never resign a lost position','how deeply serious players invest themselves in each game'],ans:3,exp:'A striking figurative statement usually stresses **depth of feeling**, here emotional investment. Literal or moral readings take the hyperbole at face value.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q80'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A philosopher observes that some gifts are not generous at all: the giver uses them to place the receiver in his debt and so assert power over him. Which example is most analogous?',choices:['A landlord who forgives late rent only for tenants who flatter him','A neighbor who shovels a stranger\'s walk and leaves unnoticed','A friend who splits a dinner bill evenly with an equal','A donor who gives anonymously to a distant flood relief fund'],ans:0,exp:'An analogy must share the **structure** (giving to create dependence and deference). Generous acts with no power imbalance lack that structure.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q81'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'On a CARS question asking which assertion the author makes with NO supporting argument or example, the best answer is a claim that:',choices:['the author illustrates with an extended example','the author simply states and then moves on from','the author defends by answering an objection','the author backs with data from a cited study'],ans:1,exp:'Look for a claim that is **asserted and left alone**. Claims backed by examples, rebuttals or data are supported, even if briefly.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q82'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author claims that among close coworkers the line between self-interest and the other person\'s interest often disappears: what helps one helps both. A coworker remarks, "We argue every week over who gets the window desk." Which claim does this remark most weaken?',choices:['Close coworkers usually enjoy working with one another','Coworkers often become friends outside the office','Close coworkers rarely have interests that pull apart','Offices with windows tend to raise worker productivity'],ans:2,exp:'The remark shows **interests diverging** between close coworkers, which weakens the claim that the line between interests disappears. Claims about enjoyment or friendship are unaffected.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q83'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Suppose it is true that membership in a choir blurs the difference between each singer\'s own interest and the interest of the group. What most likely follows?',choices:['Singers will come to sound more alike over the years','Singers will choose easier pieces to perform together','Singers will practice less often outside of rehearsals','Singers will cooperate more and compete less with one another'],ans:3,exp:'Follow the **direct consequence**: merged interests reduce rivalry and foster cooperation. Growing similarity in sound is an unrelated consequence.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q84'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'The author defines a partnership as a relationship in which each party values and supports the other. What does this definition imply?',choices:['A partnership must be reciprocal by its very nature','A partner of my partner must be my partner as well','Partnerships are strongest when they are unequal','Partnerships usually begin through shared work'],ans:0,exp:'"Each party values the other" **builds reciprocity into the definition**. Transitivity (a partner\'s partner) is a different logical property the definition never implies.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q85'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage opens by saying the relationship between medical schools and bedside manner was "complicated," then argues for four paragraphs that formal training narrowed the range of patient experience doctors attended to. The passage\'s main argument is best stated as:',choices:['The link between training and bedside manner was complicated','Schooling limited which aspects of patients doctors noticed','Medical schools ignored bedside manner throughout history','Bedside manner matters more than clinical knowledge'],ans:1,exp:'The main argument is the **claim the passage builds to**, not an introductory concession. The opening remark about complexity merely sets the stage.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q86'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'By "good taste," the author means the set of conventions that mirrored the manners of a city\'s wealthy families, not, as some claimed, a timeless standard of beauty. As used in the passage, "good taste" refers to:',choices:['a timeless standard of beauty valid across eras','personal preferences that vary from one person to another','norms that reflected an elite class\'s customs','the technical skill needed to produce fine objects'],ans:2,exp:'Use the **passage\'s own definition** of the term. The "timeless standard" reading is the view the author attributes to others and rejects.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q87'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'When a CARS question asks what kind of work the author implies is best, the answer is usually found by:',choices:['locating the values the author describes most often','picking the work the author discusses at greatest length','choosing the standard the passage says most people hold','contrasting what the author praises with what is criticized'],ans:3,exp:'The author\'s ideal emerges from **what is praised against what is criticized**, such as work that goes beyond conventions. Frequently mentioned values may be ones the author treats critically.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q88'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Eighteenth-century portraits, the author notes, closely matched the tastes of landowning families, the same families who commissioned and paid for most of them. What is the most probable reason for this match?',choices:['Painters depended on those families for their income','Painters secretly hoped to become landowners themselves','Painters admired the landowners\' superior education','Painters were usually born into landowning families'],ans:0,exp:'The passage supplies the **economic link** (the families paid), so patronage is the best-supported reason. Aspiration or admiration are motives the passage never mentions.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q89'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'An author argues that expanding a field\'s subject matter was difficult, then describes one pioneer who struggled for decades to win acceptance for a single new subject. The example most likely serves to:',choices:['prove that the field never accepted any new subjects','show how hard expansion was, even with great effort','argue that pioneers in every field face hostility','suggest that the pioneer lacked sufficient talent'],ans:1,exp:'An example\'s purpose is the **specific point it illustrates**, here the difficulty of expansion. Broader claims about all fields go beyond what the example was chosen to show.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q90'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Theory A explains a patient\'s pain solely by tissue damage; Theory B explains it solely by the patient\'s pain behaviors. The author faults Theory A for ignoring behavior and Theory B for ignoring physiology, and faults both for treating pain in isolation from the patient\'s fears, beliefs and mood. What weakness do the two theories share?',choices:['Both overlook the physiology of tissue damage','Both overlook the patient\'s outward pain behavior','Both overlook the patient\'s wider mental context','Both overlook the cost of treating chronic pain'],ans:2,exp:'A shared weakness must be **true of both theories**. Ignoring physiology or behavior applies to only one theory each, which is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q91'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Theory 1 says a mental state just is a specific brain process. Theory 2 says a mental state is defined by its role, whatever material carries it out. A researcher argues, "Octopuses release the same chemical as humans when injured, so they must feel the same pain." Which theorist would most likely make this argument?',choices:['A Theory 2 supporter, since it defines pain by its role','Both, since each theory links pain to body chemistry','A Theory 2 supporter, since it allows any physical basis','A Theory 1 supporter, since it ties states to physical bases'],ans:3,exp:'The argument **infers the same mental state from the same physical substance**, which only an identity-style view licenses. A role-based theory allows different physical bases, so shared chemistry would not settle the question for it.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q92'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage calls a theory "crude" and explains that it reads each act in isolation from the agent\'s other thoughts. Asked what the author believes about the theory, the best answer is that it:',choices:['fails to account for an agent\'s internal context','has been disproved by recent laboratory evidence','is the best available account despite its flaws','was abandoned by its founders late in their lives'],ans:0,exp:'Tie the label "crude" to the **author\'s stated reason**: neglect of internal context. The other choices are claims the author never makes.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q93'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author holds that behavior flows from many beliefs and desires working together, so no single action reveals one particular belief. Which conclusion would the author LEAST accept?',choices:['A shopper\'s purchase reflects several desires at once','A voter\'s ballot proves she holds one specific belief','Two people may act alike for very different reasons','Interpreting an action requires knowing many beliefs'],ans:1,exp:'The least acceptable conclusion **treats one action as direct proof of one belief**. The other choices are consistent with the many-beliefs view, which is why they are traps on a LEAST question.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q94'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Functionalism defines mental states by what they do, not by what they are made of. A psychologist proposes that many beliefs are not stored in advance but are built on the spot when a situation calls for them. Is this proposal consistent with functionalism?',choices:['No, because beliefs must be stored as physical structures','No, because functionalism holds that beliefs are permanent','Yes, because a belief is defined by the role it plays','Yes, because functionalism says beliefs are brain matter'],ans:2,exp:'A **function-based** definition does not care whether a belief is stored or constructed, only what it does. Reading the theory as a claim about physical substance is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q95'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'The author writes that a songbird\'s song is "socially learned": young birds wire their song circuits by listening to and answering adult tutors. As used here, "socially learned" means:',choices:['shaped by the bird\'s rank within its flock','set by the genes passed down from its parents','shaped by competition for mates each spring','formed through interaction with other birds'],ans:3,exp:'Define the term **from the sentence that explains it**: listening to and answering tutors. Rank and mating competition are social influences the passage never discusses.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q96'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'The author notes that parents of struggling children often welcome a genetic explanation for the difficulty, since it implies that nothing they did could have changed the outcome. The passage implies that parents welcome such explanations mainly to:',choices:['relieve themselves of blame for the outcome','qualify their children for special services','take part in promising new genetic research','predict the traits of their future children'],ans:0,exp:'The implied motive follows from the **stated implication** ("nothing they did could have changed it"): freedom from blame. Other motives are plausible but unstated.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q97'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author argues that musical skill depends jointly on inborn aptitude, early exposure and a supportive community. Children readily infer the rules of harmony, yet many children raised in a folk tradition harmonize in ways that conservatory teachers call wrong. Which explanation best reconciles these observations?',choices:['Children in folk traditions lack any inborn musical aptitude','Rule-inferring children still absorb their own community\'s norms','Conservatory teachers misjudge folk music out of snobbery','Folk traditions discourage children from singing at all'],ans:1,exp:'Reconcile the observations **using the author\'s multi-factor model**: aptitude lets children infer rules, but community exposure shapes which rules. Explanations outside the argument, such as snobbery, are the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q98'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A neuroscientist describes the synapses that remain in an adult brain as "the survivors of a contest of use." The metaphor implies that these synapses persist because they:',choices:['were coded by genes to last a lifetime','are larger and stronger than those lost','were used often enough to be kept','formed earliest during fetal development'],ans:2,exp:'Unpack the metaphor from its own words: a "contest of use" means **activity-dependent selection**. Genetic or structural explanations ignore the "use" in the image.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q99'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'Newborns recognize their mother\'s voice, suggesting that hearing her speak before birth helps shape early language circuits. Which advice follows most directly from this finding?',choices:['Play classical instrumental music near the pregnant belly','Have the pregnant mother practice silent sign language','Show the newborn picture books with brightly colored pages','Encourage the mother to talk aloud often during pregnancy'],ans:3,exp:'Apply the finding\'s **specific ingredient**: the mother\'s spoken voice. Activities without spoken voice, such as instrumental music, are the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q100'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Neighboring school districts compete for the best teachers by offering ever-larger signing bonuses. The author notes that bonuses rise every year while the total number of teachers in the region stays flat. What does the passage suggest about this competition?',choices:['Cooperation among districts would likely yield more overall benefit','Competition has raised the total number of teachers in the region','Signing bonuses are the best way to improve teaching quality','Districts paying the largest bonuses have the strongest schools'],ans:0,exp:'Rising costs with **no net gain** imply the competition is zero-sum, so cooperation would serve the region better. The other benefits are never claimed, and one contradicts the flat teacher count.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q101'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A passage describes rival towns competing for factories, then gives one example in which librarians across a county pooled their resources. Asked who might best foster cooperation, the answer should be drawn from:',choices:['the actors the passage depicts as driving the rivalry','the example in which leaders worked across boundaries','whoever the reader thinks holds the most authority','national officials, who are not discussed in the passage'],ans:1,exp:'Draw the answer from the passage\'s **model of success**, the cross-boundary example. Actors the passage presents as part of the problem are the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q102'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author argues that rival regions lose by competing with one another. Suppose two rival fishing villages agree to share a single harbor and both prosper. What lesson would the author most likely draw?',choices:['Rival villages should compete harder for the best harbors','Prosperity depends mainly on luck in local fish stocks','Rival communities gain by cooperating more with each other','Harbors should be controlled by a single national agency'],ans:2,exp:'A hypothetical success **confirms the author\'s thesis**, so the lesson is more cooperation. Lessons opposite to the evidence, like competing harder, are the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q103'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'An author assumes that rival groups cooperate only when economic hardship forces them to. Which study result would most directly challenge that assumption?',choices:['Groups under hardship began cooperating within a year','Groups that never faced hardship never cooperated either','Groups under hardship refused to cooperate at all','Groups began cooperating after their economies improved'],ans:3,exp:'"Only when" makes hardship **necessary**, so cooperation without hardship is the direct counterexample. Hardship without cooperation does not contradict a necessity claim.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q104'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'When three counties tried to sign a pact against luring businesses away from one another, negotiations collapsed over who would enforce it. Which conclusion does this example justify?',choices:['Controlling competition among local governments can be hard','Only national governments can regulate local competition','Counties usually refuse to cooperate on any issue at all','Business recruitment always harms the counties involved'],ans:0,exp:'One failed pact justifies only a **modest conclusion**: such control is difficult. Words like "only," "usually" and "always" claim far more than a single case can show.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q105'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'The author describes a 17th-century guild of instrument makers whose members valued careful testing over the say-so of masters, and notes that their workshops produced a stream of innovations. What relationship does the author imply?',choices:['Guild rules barred members from testing new designs','Valuing testing over authority helped drive innovation','Innovation occurred despite the guild\'s rigid traditions','The masters\' authority was the main source of new ideas'],ans:1,exp:'The juxtaposition **implies a causal link**: the testing culture fostered innovation. Barriers or rigid traditions are not described in the passage.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q106'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author characterizes a group of early naturalists as indifferent to past authority, judging claims only by observation. Which finding would most undermine this characterization?',choices:['They openly challenged the views of famous earlier scholars','They published detailed records of their own observations','They rejected findings that lacked support in ancient texts','They traveled widely to collect new specimens for study'],ans:2,exp:'Undermining evidence must **contradict the characterization**: deferring to ancient texts is the opposite of indifference to authority. Challenging earlier scholars is consistent with it, which makes it the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q107'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'An author infers that a king was indifferent to a newly founded academy. The best evidence for this inference is a detail that:',choices:['explains why kings in that era had many other priorities','describes the academy\'s early scientific achievements','reports what later historians thought of the king','shows his conduct: a charter granted and nothing more'],ans:3,exp:'The best evidence for an attitude is an **action that reveals it**, such as minimal support. Details that explain why he might have been indifferent do not show that he was.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q108'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'The established guild put up with the rival workshops springing up around it, grumbling about them now and then but never bothering to act against them. Which analogy best captures the guild\'s relationship to the rival workshops?',choices:['A dog putting up with its fleas','A hawk hunting a field mouse','A deer fleeing a wolf pack','A bird feeding its hatchlings'],ans:0,exp:'Match the **kind of relationship**: tolerated irritation without action. Predator-prey or fear analogies imply hostility or threat the passage does not describe.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q109'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A passage describes two societies of physicians around 1700 as committed to rigorous observation and experiment. It is most reasonable to suppose that neither society would have presented:',choices:['a report of dissections performed on animals','a paper predicting illness from planetary positions','a description of a newly designed surgical tool','a table of carefully recorded patient temperatures'],ans:1,exp:'Reason from the **stated character** of both groups: rigorous empiricists would not present astrology. The other items fit observation and experiment.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q110'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'A theory of music performance says that consciously learned rules, such as "keep the wrist loose," help only when a player has time, attends to form and knows the rule; in fluent playing, absorbed skill takes over. The learned rules would be most useful during:',choices:['an improvised jam session with friends','a fast passage in a live concert','a slow, deliberate technical exam','sight-reading at full performance tempo'],ans:2,exp:'Check each setting against **all three stated conditions** (time, attention to form, rule knowledge). Fast or improvised playing lacks time, so absorbed skill takes over there.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q111'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Theorist X sorts writers into underusers (who rarely revise), optimal users (who revise when it helps) and overusers (who revise so constantly they cannot finish a sentence). Theorist Y adds a category of "superwriters." A student rewrites each sentence five times and rarely finishes a draft. Under Theorist X\'s scheme, she is:',choices:['an underuser','an optimal user','a superwriter','an overuser'],ans:3,exp:'Classify using **the scheme the question names** and its definitions: constant revision that blocks completion defines an overuser. "Superwriter" belongs to a different theorist, which is the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q112'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A theory holds that studying grammar rules contributes little to real speaking ability, which comes instead from using the language. Which finding would be most troublesome for this theory?',choices:['Grammar test scores strongly predict speaking fluency','Learners who speak often become more fluent over time','Children acquire speech without formal grammar lessons','Many fluent speakers cannot state the rules they follow'],ans:0,exp:'The most troublesome finding **contradicts the core claim**: if rule knowledge predicts fluency, rules may matter after all. The other findings are ones the theory predicts.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q113'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'When a CARS question asks which information in the passage best clarifies a puzzling statement, the best answer is usually:',choices:['a true detail that mentions the same topic in passing','the point that most directly explains what the statement means','the author\'s concluding sentence, whatever its subject','a fact the reader brings from outside the passage'],ans:1,exp:'Choose the passage point with the **most explanatory power** for that statement. True but loosely related details are the trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q114'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'A soccer coach argues that players build game sense not through isolated drills but by playing real matches in which their decisions have consequences. Which practice best fits the coach\'s view?',choices:['Repeating the same passing drill for an hour','Memorizing diagrams of set plays with flashcards','Playing small-sided games against live opponents','Watching films of professional matches at home'],ans:2,exp:'Apply the theory\'s mechanism: **real use with consequences** builds the skill. Drills and memory aids are the very methods the coach discounts.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q115'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'In a historical village, a quarter of children died in their first year of life and another quarter died before age 20. Consider a parent who had children around age 20 and died at 40. Roughly what share of those children would have died before the parent?',choices:['About one-quarter','About three-quarters','Almost none','About one-half'],ans:3,exp:'**Combine both statistics**: by the parent\'s death the children are about 20, so one-quarter (infancy) plus one-quarter (before 20) gives about half. Using only the first-year figure gives the one-quarter trap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q116'},
  {s:'CARS: Foundations of Comprehension',disc:'cars',aamc:'CMP',q:'Historians report that in 1800 most deaths in a region occurred in infancy and childhood, while adults who reached 20 often lived into their sixties. This suggests that later gains in life expectancy came mainly from:',choices:['better health in infancy and early childhood','longer lives among people already elderly','changing attitudes toward aging and death','improved treatment of chronic adult disease'],ans:0,exp:'If death was **concentrated early**, the biggest room for improvement was early life. Attitudes and elderly longevity are not what the data point to.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q117'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Beyond the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RBT',q:'The author likens new residents of a co-housing community to "a crew setting sail together toward a shared harbor." The simile best supports which view?',choices:['Residents feel ambivalent about the move they have made','Residents share optimism and a common sense of purpose','Residents moved in mainly to escape their old neighbors','Residents expect the community to break up within years'],ans:1,exp:'Unpack the image: setting out **together toward a shared goal** conveys optimism and common purpose. Ambivalence or escape readings ignore the hopeful direction of the simile.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q118'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'An essay on modern aging opens with statistics showing that few people in past centuries survived to 70. The author most likely opens this way to:',choices:['show that long life is unique to one modern country','explain why older people choose to move to new homes','establish that mass survival to old age is historically new','argue that past centuries neglected their older members'],ans:2,exp:'Opening with historical data typically sets up a **contrast with the present**: widespread old age is new. Claims about one country or about reasons for moving are not supported by the data.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q119'},
  {s:'CARS: Reasoning Within the Text',disc:'cars',aamc:'RWT',q:'Since 1900, life expectancy at birth in a country has risen by 30 years, yet a 60-year-old today can expect only about 5 more years of life than a 60-year-old in 1900. Which explanation best reconciles these statistics?',choices:['Modern 60-year-olds exercise less than those of 1900','Records from 1900 overstated how long older people lived','Most of the gain came from new medicines for elderly patients','Most of the gain came from far fewer deaths in early life'],ans:3,exp:'Life expectancy at birth averages over all ages, so it rises sharply when **early deaths fall**, even if late-life gains are small. A gain driven by elderly care would have raised the 60-year-old figure, so it cannot explain the gap.',src:'aamc-cars-qpack-1:Q120'},
];
